`git commit -- <path>` commits that path's WORKING-TREE content. It bypasses
the shared index for paths it does not NAME, which is what makes this form the
right remedy in general — but the content at the path you named is theirs, so
committing now would file their work under your message, where it is durable
and no longer theirs to attribute.

You cannot narrow further: the contested path IS one you named.

